# 5. Stage-by-stage implementation detail

## 5.1 `IDLE` → `COLLECT_JIRA`

Trigger: any first message ("hi", "hello", `/start`).

Prompt rendered:

```
Hi. I'll take this Jira ticket end to end. First, the ticket details.

Please send (you can paste all at once, one per line):
  Jira key:              e.g. ABC-1234
  Summary (optional):
  Description:
  Acceptance criteria:   one per line, or numbered
```

Parsing (`collectJira.ts`):

1. Labelled-field parse. Regex for key `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]+-\d+$`.
2. If any required field missing → LLM extraction with schema `{ key, summary?, description, acceptanceCriteria: string[] }` using `response_format` JSON.
3. If still missing → re-ask only for the missing fields (keep collected ones). Show a summary table and ask "Correct? (yes / or paste corrections)".
4. On confirmation → `SELECT_REPO`.

Optional enhancement (setting `jiraDev.jira.baseUrl` + API token in secrets): if the user sends only the key, fetch `GET /rest/api/3/issue/{key}` and pre-fill description/AC (Atlassian Document Format → plaintext). Ask the user to confirm the fetched text.

## 5.2 `SELECT_REPO` → `SWITCHING_WORKSPACE`

Prompt:

```
Which local repository should I work in?
Send the absolute path (e.g. /Users/me/work/payments-service or C:\work\payments-service),
or click "Browse…".
```

Button: `stream.button({ command: 'jiraDev.browseRepo', title: 'Browse…' })` → `vscode.window.showOpenDialog({ canSelectFolders: true })`.

Validation (`workspace.ts`):

1. `fs.stat(path)` is a directory.
2. `git -C <path> rev-parse --show-toplevel` succeeds; normalize `repo.path` to the toplevel.
3. `git remote get-url origin` (store; warn if none — push will fail later).
4. Detect build tool (`buildToolDetector.ts`): `pom.xml` → maven (`./mvnw` if exists else `mvn`); `build.gradle(.kts)` → gradle (`./gradlew` else `gradle`); `package.json` → npm/pnpm/yarn by lockfile. Store `buildCommand`/`runCommand` defaults (overridable by settings):
   - maven: build `mvn -B clean install -DskipTests=false`; run `mvn spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.profiles=dev`
   - gradle: build `./gradlew clean build`; run `./gradlew bootRun --args='--spring.profiles.active=dev'`
   - npm: build `npm ci && npm run build`; run `npm run start:dev` if exists else `npm start`
5. Detect port: parse `src/main/resources/application-dev.{yml,yaml,properties}` then `application.*` for `server.port`; default 8080. Also scan `management.server.port` and `management.endpoints.web.base-path`.

Workspace switch logic:

- If `repo.path` is already a workspace folder → skip switching, go to `COLLECT_GIT_STRATEGY`.
- Else, if the current window has a workspace open: call `vscode.workspace.updateWorkspaceFolders(0, count, { uri })` to **replace** folders in-place. In a single-folder window this still triggers a reload; in a multi-root/untitled workspace it does not. So:
  - Save session with `state = SWITCHING_WORKSPACE` first.
  - Show `stream.markdown('Switching workspace to <path>. VS Code may reload; I will offer to resume.')`.
  - Execute `vscode.commands.executeCommand('vscode.openFolder', uri, { forceNewWindow: false })`.
- On reactivation the resume hook in §4.4 fires. `SWITCHING_WORKSPACE.onEnter` verifies the folder matches and advances to `COLLECT_GIT_STRATEGY`.

## 5.3 `COLLECT_GIT_STRATEGY` → `GIT_PREPARE`

Prompt (pre-filled with suggestions):

```
Git strategy. Current branches: main, develop, release/2026.09  (current: develop)

  Base branch:      [suggest: develop]
  New branch name:  [suggest: feature/ABC-1234-short-slug]
  Commit message:   [suggest: ABC-1234: <summary>]

Reply with the three values (or "use suggestions").
```

Suggestions come from `git branch -r` (prefer `develop` > `main` > `master`), Jira key + slugified summary, and a configurable template `jiraDev.git.commitTemplate` (`{key}: {summary}`).

Validation:

- Base branch exists on `origin` (`git ls-remote --heads origin <base>`). If not, list candidates and re-ask.
- Feature branch name passes `git check-ref-format --branch <name>`. If it already exists locally or remotely, ask: reuse / choose another.
- Commit message non-empty; enforce Jira key present (prepend if missing, tell the user).

Confirm table → `GIT_PREPARE`.

## 5.4 `GIT_PREPARE` (automated)

Exact command sequence (`git.ts`), each logged to the output channel with exit code and duration:

```bash
git -C <repo> status --porcelain
#   if non-empty:
#     allowDirtyTree=abort   → stop, tell user to commit/stash; stay in COLLECT_GIT_STRATEGY
#     allowDirtyTree=stash   → git stash push -u -m "jira-dev/<key>/<timestamp>"
#     allowDirtyTree=discard → require explicit "yes, discard" reply, then git reset --hard && git clean -fd
git -C <repo> fetch --all --prune --tags
git -C <repo> pull --ff-only                       # "after switching to repo do a git pull" (on current branch; failure is non-fatal, logged)
git -C <repo> checkout <base>                      # creates tracking branch if needed: checkout -b <base> origin/<base>
git -C <repo> reset --hard origin/<base>           # the "force pull" — makes local base identical to remote
git -C <repo> pull --ff-only origin <base>         # no-op sanity check
git -C <repo> rev-parse HEAD                       # store git.baseSha
git -C <repo> checkout -b <feature>                # or checkout <feature> && rebase onto base if reusing
```

Failure handling: any non-zero exit → `recordFailure` with the last 60 lines of git output → `FAILED_AWAIT_REDO`. Common cases surfaced with tailored messages: auth failure on fetch (suggest credential helper), branch not found, conflict on rebase when reusing branch.

Success → `ANALYZE_AND_PLAN`.

## 5.5 `ANALYZE_AND_PLAN` (automated) — "go through the entire code and prepare the plan"

This is the most complex automated stage. It has four sub-steps with `stream.progress()` updates.

### 5.5.1 Index the codebase (`indexer.ts`)

- Walk the repo honoring `.gitignore` (use `git ls-files` plus `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` for untracked); skip binaries, `target/`, `build/`, `node_modules/`, files > 400 KB.
- For each file: path, size, language (by extension), line count. For Java/Kotlin: extract package, class names, annotations (`@RestController`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Entity`, `@Configuration`) with a lightweight regex pass — enough for routing, no full parse.
- Produce a **repo map**: a compact tree with per-file one-line summaries (class names, annotations, public method signatures for Java via regex `public\s+[\w<>\[\], ]+\s+(\w+)\s*\(`). Typical Spring service compresses to 5–15k tokens.
- Cache index at `.jira-dev/index.json` keyed by `git rev-parse HEAD`.

### 5.5.2 Select relevant files (`contextBuilder.ts`)

1. Extract keywords from Jira description + AC (LLM call: "return 10–20 identifiers, domain nouns, endpoint paths, config keys").
2. `rg -n -i --json <keyword>` across the repo for each keyword; score files by hits × keyword weight.
3. Always include: build file(s), `application*.yml/properties`, main application class, any file with `@RestController` if the ticket mentions API/endpoint, DB migrations dir listing if ticket mentions schema.
4. Fill a token budget (default 60k for the planner) with top-scored files, full content; add repo map for the rest.

### 5.5.3 Generate the plan (`planner.md` prompt)

System prompt contract (abbreviated):

```
You are a senior engineer planning a change in an existing codebase. You will receive:
- Jira key, description, acceptance criteria
- A repo map and the full content of relevant files
Produce a plan as JSON matching the provided schema. Rules:
- Reference only files that exist or that you explicitly mark as "create".
- Each step must be concrete: file, what changes, why, how it maps to an acceptance criterion.
- Include test changes (unit/integration) for every behavioural change.
- Include config/migration/build changes if needed.
- List assumptions and open questions separately. Do not invent requirements.
- Estimate risk per step: low/medium/high with one-line justification.
```

`PlanSchema`:

```ts
const PlanSchema = z.object({
  overview: z.string(),
  assumptions: z.array(z.string()),
  openQuestions: z.array(z.string()),
  steps: z.array(z.object({
    id: z.number(),
    title: z.string(),
    files: z.array(z.object({ path: z.string(), action: z.enum(['modify', 'create', 'delete']) })),
    details: z.string(),
    acceptanceCriteriaRefs: z.array(z.number()),
    risk: z.enum(['low', 'medium', 'high']),
    riskNote: z.string(),
  })),
  testing: z.array(z.string()),
  rollback: z.string(),
});
```

Post-validation: every `modify`/`delete` path must exist (check against index); otherwise re-prompt the model once with the list of invalid paths.

If `attempt > 1` (redo after failure), the prompt also receives `session.failures` (last failure's stage, message, log excerpt) and the previous plan, with the instruction "revise the plan to address this failure".

### 5.5.4 Render and hand off

Render the plan as markdown in the chat: overview, table of steps (id / title / files / AC refs / risk), testing, assumptions, open questions. Save `.jira-dev/plan-v<N>.md`. Transition to `AWAIT_PLAN_APPROVAL`.

## 5.6 `AWAIT_PLAN_APPROVAL` (input)

Prompt with buttons:

```
Do you approve this plan?
[Approve]  [Request changes]  [Open plan file]
Or reply "approve", or "changes: <what to change>".
```

- `APPROVE` → set `plan.approvedAt` → `IMPLEMENT`.
- `REJECT` / `changes: ...` → append feedback to `plan.feedbackHistory`, re-enter `ANALYZE_AND_PLAN` with feedback in the prompt, bump `plan.version`.
- If the plan has `openQuestions`, the approval prompt shows them first and asks the user to answer; answers are appended to `jira.description` under "Clarifications" and the plan regenerated before approval is accepted (setting `jiraDev.plan.requireAnswers`, default true).

## 5.7 `IMPLEMENT` (automated) — agentic edit loop

Design: a bounded tool-calling loop (`implement.ts`) using the `implementer.md` prompt and the tools in `services/tools/`.

Tools exposed to the model (JSON schema each):

| Tool | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `list_dir(path)` | Directory listing (respecting ignore rules) |
| `read_file(path, startLine?, endLine?)` | Returns content with line numbers |
| `search(pattern, glob?)` | ripgrep, max 200 hits |
| `write_file(path, content)` | Create/overwrite; recorded in `filesChanged` |
| `apply_patch(path, edits: [{oldText, newText}])` | Exact-match replacement; fails loudly if `oldText` not unique |
| `run_command(cmd, timeoutSec)` | Whitelisted commands only: `mvn`, `./mvnw`, `gradle`, `./gradlew`, `npm`, `npx`, `git status/diff`; cwd = repo |
| `mark_step_done(stepId, summary)` | Progress tracking |
| `finish(summary)` | Ends the loop |

Loop:

```
messages = [system(implementer.md), user(jira + approved plan JSON + repo map)]
for i in 1..maxIterations:
    resp = llm.chat(messages, tools)
    if resp.toolCalls is empty and contains finish → break
    for call in resp.toolCalls:
        result = execute(call)         # every write goes through vscode.WorkspaceEdit so it's undoable
        messages.push(toolResult(call.id, result))
    stream.progress(`Step ${doneSteps}/${plan.steps.length} …`)
    if token.isCancellationRequested → abort, leave working tree as-is, → FAILED_AWAIT_REDO
if loop exhausted without finish → failure "implementation did not converge"
```

Guardrails:

- Writes restricted to inside `repo.path`; refuse paths containing `..` or outside toplevel; refuse `.git/`.
- After loop: `git diff --stat` and `git status --porcelain` are rendered in the chat as a collapsible file list (`stream.filetree`) with clickable diffs (`vscode.diff` against `HEAD`).
- Store `implementation.filesChanged`, `iterations`, `summary`.
- All steps in the plan must have `mark_step_done`; if not, one follow-up prompt "Steps X, Y not marked done — complete or explain", then proceed.

→ `BUILD`.

## 5.8 `BUILD` (automated) — "ensure that the clean build is successful"

1. Command: `repo.buildCommand` (overridable). Run via `process.ts` with `spawn(shell=true, cwd=repo)`, env inherits user env plus `CI=true`, `MAVEN_OPTS`, `JAVA_HOME` from settings if provided.
2. Stream stdout/stderr to the output channel and a pseudoterminal tab titled `Jira Dev: build`. Also tee to `.jira-dev/logs/build-<attempt>.log`.
3. Timeout `jiraDev.build.timeoutSeconds` (default 1200). Kill process tree on timeout via `tree-kill`.
4. Exit code 0 → record `build.success=true` → `RUN_AND_HEALTHCHECK`.
5. Exit code ≠ 0 → **fix loop** (max `jiraDev.build.maxFixAttempts`, default 3):
   - Extract errors: for maven, lines matching `\[ERROR\]` plus the `COMPILATION ERROR` block and surefire `Tests run:.*Failures: [1-9]` summaries plus `FAILED` test names; for gradle, `e: ` / `error:` lines and `> Task .* FAILED`; for npm, tsc/eslint errors.
   - Call LLM with `buildFixer.md` prompt: errors + contents of referenced files → tool loop (same tools) limited to 10 iterations.
   - Re-run build.
6. Fix attempts exhausted → `FAILED_AWAIT_REDO` with the error excerpt.

## 5.9 `RUN_AND_HEALTHCHECK` (automated) — "make application up and point to dev and test health/actuator endpoint"

1. Start: `repo.runCommand` with env `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=dev` (also passed as arg for Spring; for npm `NODE_ENV=dev` / `APP_ENV=dev`). Spawned detached, PID tree tracked; stdout tee'd to `.jira-dev/logs/run.log` and a `Jira Dev: app` terminal.
2. Readiness: poll `http://localhost:<port><healthPath>` every 3 s until `jiraDev.health.timeoutSeconds`. Accept 200 with JSON `status == "UP"`; if body isn't JSON, accept any 2xx. If `management.server.port` differs, use that. If `management.endpoints.web.base-path` is customized, respect it.
3. Log scanning while waiting: if run.log matches `APPLICATION FAILED TO START`, `Port .* was already in use`, `BindException`, `Exception in thread "main"` → fail early with the excerpt. For port-in-use, offer the user "kill process on port and retry" (requires explicit yes).
4. On success: store `health` (url, status, body, timestamp), render `✓ GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/health → 200 {"status":"UP"}` in chat.
5. Always stop the app afterwards (`tree-kill(pid, 'SIGTERM')`, then SIGKILL after 10 s) unless setting `jiraDev.run.keepRunning=true`.
6. Failure → `FAILED_AWAIT_REDO`.

## 5.10 `COMMIT_AND_PUSH` (automated)

```bash
git -C <repo> add -A                              # respects .gitignore; .jira-dev excluded via .git/info/exclude
git -C <repo> commit -m "<commitMessage>"         # if pre-commit hook fails → failure with hook output
git -C <repo> push -u origin <feature>            # retry 4× with backoff 4/8/16/32 s on network errors only
git -C <repo> rev-parse HEAD                      # git.pushedSha
```

Before committing, show `git diff --stat` and, if `jiraDev.git.confirmBeforePush` is true (default true), ask "Push N files to origin/<feature>? [Push] [Cancel]". The user asked for auto-push after success; the default confirmation is a safety valve they can switch off.

If the remote returns a PR-creation URL in push output (`remote: Create a pull request…`), render it as a link.

→ `AWAIT_DEV_DEPLOY`.

## 5.11 `AWAIT_DEV_DEPLOY` (input)

```
Pushed <sha7> to origin/<feature>.
Please deploy this branch to the dev environment. Reply "done" (optionally with a build/deploy URL or note) when it is up.
[Deployed]
```

`DONE` → store `deploy.confirmedAt`, note → `AWAIT_TEST_RUNNER_URL`. Anything else → gentle re-ask; `FAIL`/"deploy failed" → `FAILED_AWAIT_REDO` with the user's note as the failure message.

## 5.12 `AWAIT_TEST_RUNNER_URL` (input)

```
Please start the test runner application locally and send me its URL (e.g. http://localhost:3000).
```

Validation: parse URL; `fetch(url, { method: 'GET' })` with 5 s timeout; accept any HTTP response (even 401/302). If unreachable, say so and re-ask. Store `testRunner.url` → `COLLECT_LOGIN_CREDS`.

## 5.13 `COLLECT_LOGIN_CREDS` and `COLLECT_CLIENT_CREDS` (input, secret-safe)

Chat input is plain text and is logged by VS Code's chat history, so **passwords must not be typed into the chat**. Flow:

```
I need your kID and password for the test runner. Click the button below; the password is entered in a secure input box and stored encrypted in VS Code's SecretStorage for this session only.
[Enter kID & password]
```

`jiraDev.enterSecret` command:

- `showInputBox({ prompt: 'kID', ignoreFocusOut: true })`
- `showInputBox({ prompt: 'Password for kID', password: true, ignoreFocusOut: true })`
- `context.secrets.store('jiraDev.<sessionId>.kidPassword', value)`; store only the key name in `session.secretKeys`.
- Then post a synthetic `DONE` message into the engine → `COLLECT_CLIENT_CREDS` with the same pattern for clientId / client secret.

If the user does type a password into the chat anyway: detect (state is a credential state and message isn't an intent), store it, and reply "Stored. For safety, please clear that message from chat history." — never echo it.

→ `BROWSER_REGRESSION`.

## 5.14 `BROWSER_REGRESSION` (automated) — Playwright

### 5.14.1 Test runner profile (`resources/testRunnerProfiles/default.json`)

Because the test-runner UI is unknown, all selectors live in a profile:

```json
{
  "name": "default",
  "login": {
    "path": "/login",
    "userSelector": "input[name='username'], #kid",
    "passwordSelector": "input[type='password']",
    "submitSelector": "button[type='submit']",
    "successSelector": "[data-testid='dashboard'], nav"
  },
  "clientAuth": {
    "path": "/settings/client",
    "clientIdSelector": "input[name='clientId']",
    "clientSecretSelector": "input[name='clientSecret']",
    "submitSelector": "button:has-text('Save'), button:has-text('Connect')",
    "successSelector": "text=Connected"
  },
  "regression": {
    "apiSectionPath": "/api",
    "apiNavSelector": "a:has-text('API')",
    "runButtonSelector": "button:has-text('Run Regression'), button:has-text('Run All')",
    "runningIndicatorSelector": ".spinner, text=Running",
    "completeSelector": "text=Completed, .status-complete",
    "timeoutSeconds": 3600,
    "summarySelectors": { "total": ".summary .total", "passed": ".summary .passed", "failed": ".summary .failed" }
  },
  "download": {
    "buttonSelector": "button:has-text('Download'), a:has-text('Export')",
    "expectedExtensions": [".xlsx", ".csv", ".json", ".html", ".zip"]
  }
}
```

Profiles are looked up in `<repo>/.jira-dev/profiles/<name>.json`, then user global storage, then the bundled default. The `jiraDev.recordProfile` command launches `npx playwright codegen <url>` and explains how to copy selectors into a profile; on first run against an unknown runner the agent asks the user to confirm the profile before proceeding.

### 5.14.2 Automation sequence (`browser/playwright.ts`)

```ts
const browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: false, slowMo: 50 });
const context = await browser.newContext({ acceptDownloads: true, viewport: { width: 1400, height: 900 } });
const page = await context.newPage();

// 1. Login (kID)
await page.goto(url + profile.login.path);
await page.fill(profile.login.userSelector, kid);
await page.fill(profile.login.passwordSelector, kidPassword);
await page.click(profile.login.submitSelector);
await page.waitForSelector(profile.login.successSelector, { timeout: 60_000 });

// 2. Client credentials
if (profile.clientAuth.path) await page.goto(url + profile.clientAuth.path);
await page.fill(profile.clientAuth.clientIdSelector, clientId);
await page.fill(profile.clientAuth.clientSecretSelector, clientSecret);
await page.click(profile.clientAuth.submitSelector);
await page.waitForSelector(profile.clientAuth.successSelector, { timeout: 60_000 });

// 3. Go to API section and run regression
await page.goto(url + profile.regression.apiSectionPath)  /* or click apiNavSelector */;
await page.click(profile.regression.runButtonSelector);
await page.waitForSelector(profile.regression.completeSelector, { timeout: profile.regression.timeoutSeconds * 1000 });

// 4. Read summary counts if selectors present
```

Between each step: `stream.progress('Logged in as <kid>…')`, and a screenshot saved to `.jira-dev/results/<key>/screenshots/<step>.png`. Secrets are pulled from `context.secrets` immediately before `fill` and never logged; the process-log redactor (§7) also masks them.

Error handling: selector timeout → screenshot + page HTML snippet saved → ask the user in chat: "I could not find <element>. Options: [I'll do this step manually — continue], [Update profile], [Fail]". The browser stays open so the user can complete a step manually and reply "continue"; the automation resumes from the next step. This manual-fallback is what makes an unknown UI tractable.

→ `DOWNLOAD_RESULTS`.

## 5.15 `DOWNLOAD_RESULTS` (automated)

```ts
const [download] = await Promise.all([
  page.waitForEvent('download', { timeout: 120_000 }),
  page.click(profile.download.buttonSelector),
]);
const target = path.join(repo, resultsDir, jiraKey, `${timestamp}-${download.suggestedFilename()}`);
await download.saveAs(target);
```

- If the result is `.zip`, extract alongside. If `.json`/`.csv`/`.xlsx`, attempt to parse a summary (`total/passed/failed/skipped`) with tolerant heuristics; otherwise use the on-page summary selectors; otherwise leave `summary` undefined.
- Save a final full-page screenshot.
- Close the browser unless failures are present (keep it open for inspection, tell the user).
- Render: file paths (clickable), summary if known.

→ `AWAIT_TEST_VERDICT`.

## 5.16 `AWAIT_TEST_VERDICT` (input)

```
Regression complete: 148 total, 146 passed, 2 failed (from downloaded results).
Did the regression pass for this change? [Pass] [Fail — redo from planning]
```

If summary shows `failed > 0`, default suggestion is Fail but the user decides (flaky tests exist). `PASS` → `ASK_REPORT_FORMAT`. `FAIL` → append failure with the failed test names → `FAILED_AWAIT_REDO`.

## 5.17 `ASK_REPORT_FORMAT` → `DOCUMENT`

"Document everything as Markdown, Word (.docx), or both? [Markdown] [Word] [Both]" (skipped if `jiraDev.report.defaultFormat` isn't `ask`).

`DOCUMENT` generates `<repo>/docs/<KEY>-implementation-report.md` and/or `.docx` (and copies to `.jira-dev/results/<KEY>/`). Sections:

1. Header: Jira key, summary, date, engineer (git `user.name`), session id, attempt count.
2. Jira description and acceptance criteria (with per-AC ✓ mapping from plan steps).
3. Repository and git: repo path, remote, base branch + `baseSha`, feature branch, commit message, `pushedSha`, PR link if any.
4. Approved plan (final version) and plan revision history (feedback given).
5. Implementation: files changed (`git diff --stat baseSha..pushedSha`), per-step summaries.
6. Build: command, attempts, duration, result; link to log file; fix-loop summary if any.
7. Runtime verification: run command, profile, health URL, response, timestamp.
8. Deployment: user confirmation time and note.
9. Regression: test runner URL (secrets omitted), profile used, start/end, summary counts, result file paths, screenshots embedded (docx) / linked (md).
10. Failures and redo history (from `session.failures`).
11. Appendix: full command log (redacted).

Markdown via template literals; docx via `docx` package (`Document`, `Paragraph`, `Table`, `ImageRun`). Ask whether to commit the report to the branch (`[Commit & push report] [Leave uncommitted]`); if yes, `git add docs/… && git commit -m "<key>: add implementation report" && git push`.

→ `AWAIT_LOG_VALIDATION`.

## 5.18 `AWAIT_LOG_VALIDATION` → `CLOSED`

```
Report written: docs/ABC-1234-implementation-report.md
Please validate the logs: [Open agent log] [Open build log] [Open app log] [Open results folder]
Reply "validated" to close the session, or "issue: <text>" to record a problem.
```

- `validated`/`DONE` → `CLOSED`: delete all `jiraDev.<sessionId>.*` secrets, archive session JSON to `.jira-dev/sessions/<id>.json`, clear `globalState`, print final summary.
- `issue:` → appended to failures; ask whether to redo from planning or close anyway.

## 5.19 `FAILED_AWAIT_REDO` (input) — the universal failure handler

Entered from any automated stage via `recordFailure`. Renders:

```
✗ Failed at <STAGE>: <message>
Last log lines:
  …
Options: [Redo from planning] [Retry this step] [Abort session]
```

- `REDO` → `attempt++`, keep Jira/repo/git config, discard `plan.approvedAt`, → `ANALYZE_AND_PLAN` (with failure context; §5.5.3). Working tree is left as-is so the planner/implementer can build on partial work; if the user prefers a clean slate they can reply "redo clean" → `git checkout -- . && git clean -fd` after explicit confirmation.
- `RETRY` → re-enter the failed state (useful for transient network/build issues).
- `ABORT` → `CLOSED` without report; secrets wiped.

This satisfies "if anything fails you have to redo the steps from planning" while leaving the user in control.

